options(ospca.threads = 2, width = 80)
@

\section{Introduction}

Principal component analysis (PCA) is a bidirectional method, as a direction
and its negative describe the same principal component. Many data sets,
however, are asymmetric, and the two half-lines of a direction can then carry
quite different information. One-sided (directional) PCA (Hallin, Nordhausen
and Radojicic) therefore considers each half-line separately. In this vignette
we illustrate the linear version, \code{ospca()}, together with its helper
functions and methods, on a simple simulated data set. The nonlinear version is
described in \code{vignette("nlospca", package = "ospca")}, and the independent
nonlinear component analysis of Gunsilius and Schennach (2023) in
\code{vignette("gspca", package = "ospca")}.

\section{The one-sided objective}

Let $X$ be a centered random vector in $\mathbb{R}^d$ and $u$ a unit vector.
The positive part of the projection of $X$ onto $u$,
\[
\langle u, X \rangle^+ = \max(\langle u, X \rangle, 0),
\]
is the projection if $X$ lies on the side of $u$ and zero otherwise. The
importance of the direction $u$ is measured by the one-sided objective
\[
J(u) = E\big[(\langle u, X \rangle^+)^2\big],
\]
that is, by the second moment of the positive part of the projection. Since
\[
\langle u, X \rangle^2 = (\langle u, X \rangle^+)^2 + (\langle -u, X \rangle^+)^2,
\]
the variance criterion of classical PCA is $J(u) + J(-u)$, the sum of the
objectives of the two half-lines of a direction. For a symmetric distribution,
$J(u) = J(-u)$ and it makes no difference whether $J(u)$ or $J(u) + J(-u)$ is
maximized. For an asymmetric distribution, however, the two half-lines can have
quite different objectives, and the direction that maximizes their sum need not
maximize either of them.

The first one-sided principal direction $u_1$ maximizes $J(u)$ over the unit
sphere. Each further direction $u_i$ maximizes $J(u)$ over the unit vectors
orthogonal to $u_1, \ldots, u_{i-1}$ and over the antipodes of
$u_1, \ldots, u_{i-1}$ that have not been chosen yet. This gives up to $2d$
directions, and the one-sided principal components (the scores) are the
positive parts $\langle u_i, X \rangle^+$. In the sample, the expectation is
replaced by the average over the centered observations.

\section{Data}

We simulate a bivariate data set that consists of an elliptical cloud of 300
observations and a skewed tail of 100 observations, which points at an angle of
60 degrees.

<<data>>=
set.seed(1)
n1 <- 300; n2 <- 100
r <- 1 + rexp(n2, rate = 1/3)
X <- rbind(cbind(rnorm(n1, sd = 2), rnorm(n1)),
           cbind(r * cos(pi / 3), r * sin(pi / 3)) +
             matrix(rnorm(2 * n2, sd = 0.3), ncol = 2))
colnames(X) <- c("x1", "x2")
@

\section{Fitting the model}

<<fit>>=
library(ospca)
fit <- ospca(X)
fit
@

By default, the data are centered by their mean (argument \code{center}) and
all $2d = 4$ directions are kept (argument \code{n\_components}). The
directions, in the columns, and the scores are extracted by \code{directions()}
and \code{scores()}:

<<directions>>=
directions(fit)
head(scores(fit))
@

Note that the second direction is the antipode of the first. The half-line
$-u_1$ thus has a smaller objective than $u_1$, but a larger one than the two
half-lines orthogonal to $u_1$. Note also that an observation has a positive
score for at most one of the two directions $u_1$ and $-u_1$.

The summary gives the objectives and the diagnostics of the orthonormal axes on
which the directions are based (see Section~\ref{sec:helpers}):

<<summary>>=
summary(fit)
@

\section{Comparison with classical PCA}

The first one-sided direction follows the tail, whereas the first principal
component is a compromise between the tail and the elongated cloud, as it also
accounts for the opposite half-line:

<<angles>>=
pc <- prcomp(X)
angle <- function(u) (atan2(u[[2]], u[[1]]) * 180 / pi) %% 360
angle(directions(fit)[, 1])
angle(pc$rotation[, 1]) %% 180    # the sign of a principal component is arbitrary
@

The objective is computed by \code{os\_objective()}, which uses the data as they
are, so that we center them first. The objectives of $u_1$ and $-u_1$ add up to
the variance of the projections onto $u_1$:

<<objective>>=
Xc <- scale(X, center = TRUE, scale = FALSE)
u1 <- directions(fit)[, 1]
c(J_u1 = os_objective(u1, Xc), J_minus_u1 = os_objective(-u1, Xc),
  sum = os_objective(u1, Xc) + os_objective(-u1, Xc),
  variance = mean((Xc %*% u1)^2))
@

The objective as a function of the angle of $u$ in Figure~\ref{fig:objective-plot}
shows the asymmetry: the curve of $J(-u)$ (dashed) is the curve of $J(u)$
shifted by 180 degrees, and their sum (dotted) is the variance criterion of
classical PCA. The points mark the four one-sided directions.

<<objective-plot, fig.height = 3.6, fig.cap = "One-sided objective $J(u)$ (solid), $J(-u)$ (dashed) and their sum (dotted) as functions of the angle of $u$, with the four one-sided directions.">>=
theta <- seq(0, 360, by = 1)
U <- cbind(cos(theta * pi / 180), sin(theta * pi / 180))
J <- apply(U, 1, function(u) os_objective(u, Xc))
J_minus <- apply(-U, 1, function(u) os_objective(u, Xc))
plot(theta, J + J_minus, type = "l", lty = 3, xlab = "angle of u (degrees)",
     ylab = "objective", ylim = c(0, max(J + J_minus)))
lines(theta, J)
lines(theta, J_minus, lty = 2)
points(apply(directions(fit), 2, angle), fit$objective, pch = 19)
text(apply(directions(fit), 2, angle), fit$objective, 1:4, pos = 3)
@

\section{Helper functions}\label{sec:helpers}

Besides \code{os\_objective()}, there are two helper functions.
\code{os\_project()} gives the positive parts of the projections onto a
direction, which for the fitted directions are the scores:

<<project>>=
head(os_project(u1, Xc))
all.equal(os_project(u1, Xc), unname(scores(fit)[, 1]))
@

\code{os\_axes()} computes the greedy one-sided orthonormal axes
$a_1, \ldots, a_d$ of centered data: $a_1$ maximizes $J(u)$ over the unit
sphere, and each further axis maximizes it over the unit vectors orthogonal to
the previous axes. \code{ospca()} then evaluates the objective for the $2d$
candidates $\pm a_1, \ldots, \pm a_d$ and keeps those with the largest
objectives, in decreasing order.

<<axes>>=
A <- os_axes(Xc)
A[, ]                        # the axes, in the columns
attr(A, "diagnostics")
@

Since $J(u)$ is maximized over the sphere, it can have several local maxima.
Each axis is therefore found by a multi-start search, in which the best
\code{n\_polish} of \code{n\_starts} random starting directions are refined by
a generalized power iteration (\code{method = "power"}, the default) or by a
quasi-Newton optimizer (\code{method = "bfgs"}). The diagnostics give, for each
axis, the attained objective and the relative gap to the best sampled direction
that is more than 45 degrees away. A gap below \code{tol\_tie} means that a
clearly different direction does almost as well, that is, the maximizer is
effectively not unique. Note that the gaps vary somewhat between runs, as they
are based on the random starting directions.

\section{Graphical summaries}

For bivariate data, \code{plot()} draws the centered data with an arrow for
each direction, with length proportional to the square root of the objective.
For more variables, it plots two chosen columns of the scores against each
other. \code{screeplot()} shows the objectives (bars) together with the
reconstruction errors (line) described in Section~\ref{sec:reconstruction};
with \code{type = "objective"} or \code{type = "mse"} only one of them is drawn.
Both are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:plots}.

<<plots, fig.cap = "Data with the one-sided directions (left) and scree plot (right).">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
plot(fit)
screeplot(fit)
@

\section{New observations and reconstruction}\label{sec:reconstruction}

\code{predict()} gives the one-sided scores of new observations, which are
centered by the fitted center. Each of the following four points has its
largest score for a different direction, the point in the tail for the first
one:

<<predict>>=
Xnew <- rbind(c(4, 7), c(-3, -4), c(4, -2), c(-2, 2))
predict(fit, Xnew)
@

The reconstruction with the first $k$ directions is the center plus
\[
\sum_{i \le k} \langle u_i, X \rangle^+ u_i,
\]
and \code{reconstruction\_mse()} gives the mean squared reconstruction error:

<<reconstruct>>=
sapply(1:4, function(k) reconstruction_mse(X, reconstruct(fit, n_components = k)))
@

With all four directions the reconstruction is exact. With the first two
directions, $u_1$ and $-u_1$, an observation is replaced by its orthogonal
projection onto the line spanned by $u_1$. The error of this projection is
slightly larger than that of the first principal component, which minimizes
it:

<<pca-mse>>=
Xhat_pc <- sweep(pc$x[, 1, drop = FALSE] %*% t(pc$rotation[, 1, drop = FALSE]),
                 2, pc$center, "+")
reconstruction_mse(X, Xhat_pc)
@

\code{fitted()} and \code{residuals()} give the reconstructions and the data
minus their reconstructions:

<<fitted>>=
head(fitted(fit, n_components = 2))
head(residuals(fit, n_components = 2))
@

\section{Ties}

For a centrally symmetric sample, $J(u) = J(-u)$ for all $u$. Each direction
and its antipode are then tied, as in classical PCA, and \code{ospca()} warns
that their order is arbitrary:

<<ties>>=
set.seed(2)
Z <- matrix(rnorm(400), ncol = 2) %*% diag(c(2, 1))
fit_sym <- ospca(rbind(Z, -Z))
fit_sym$objective
@

